# The model as one agent sees it.
#
# The state holds one model for the whole system, which is what makes a group's
# knowledge expressible at all: "r1 knows that r2 does not know" is a statement
# about one structure, not about two. But a deployment often needs the other
# reading — what would this robot act on, given only what it has seen — and
# that is a different model: the worlds this agent considers possible, taken
# from the ones that are actually the case.
#
# Under S5 the agent's perspective always includes the actual world, so it can
# be ignorant but never wrong. Under KD45 it need not, which is what lets an
# agent be mistaken rather than merely uninformed.

# The agent whose point of view is wanted.
string agent
---
bool success
string error

# The shape of that agent's model.
uint32 worlds
uint32 designated

# The perspective itself, in the shape the task format gives an initial state,
# so it can be planned from or handed to another node.
string model

# True when the agent considers the actual world possible. False means it holds
# a belief the world contradicts, which S5 cannot express and KD45 can.
bool includes_actual
